iption"} [:p {} "Germany, East or unified, had won the K-4 500 at 17 of the last 20 editions of the World Championships, and were the defending Olympic champions, but had lost the World title in 1999 to their long-time rival Hungary. These two nations won their heats in the opening round and led the rest of the pack by a significant margin in the final, which Germany won by 0.414 seconds, leaving Hungary with the silver medal. Two members of Germany’s crew, " [:a {:href "/athletes/90193"} "Katrin Wagner"] " and the great " [:a {:href "/athletes/9879"} "Birgit Fischer"] ", became double Olympic champions by emerging victorious in the K-2 500 the following day, while two of the Hungarians, " [:a {:href "/athletes/90202"} "Katalin Kovács"] " and " [:a {:href "/athletes/90205"} "Szilvia Szabó"] ", were silver medalists in the same event. Romania, meanwhile, the 1997 European champion, edged out Poland, the 1999 World runner-up, by 0.066 seconds to take bronze."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitors"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "SF"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "Germany"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/GER"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/GER.png"}] "GER"]] [:td {} "1:33.895 (1 h2)"] [:td {} "–"] [:td {} "1:34.532 (1)"] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {:colspan "11"} [:a {:href "/athletes/9879"} "Birgit Fischer"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/9871"} "Manuela Mucke"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/9880"} "Anett Schuck"] " • " [:a {:href "/athletes/90193"} "Katrin Wagner"]]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} "Hungary"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: